1
00:00:16,270 --> 00:00:18,350
[ Thunder Rumbles ]

2
00:00:20,310 --> 00:00:22,600
[ Thunderclap ]

3
00:03:12,070 --> 00:03:15,190
[ Motor Whirring ]

4
00:03:15,280 --> 00:03:18,240
[ Motor Stops ] 
[ Shouting ] 
See how it's done?

5
00:03:18,320 --> 00:03:22,370
Yeah, I think I got 
the hang of it. 
You better.

6
00:03:22,410 --> 00:03:27,040
Boss'll chew my head off 
if these cores get messed up. 
Don't worry.

7
00:03:41,640 --> 00:03:43,810
We're at 26 feet.

8
00:03:43,890 --> 00:03:46,060
You let Jason 
operate the drill?

9
00:03:46,140 --> 00:03:48,100
Yeah, he can 
handle it.

10
00:04:11,540 --> 00:04:15,920
[ Motor Slows, Stops ]

11
00:04:15,960 --> 00:04:18,590
[ Crunching ]

12
00:04:18,630 --> 00:04:21,590
[ Cracking ]

13
00:04:38,740 --> 00:04:40,950
I didn't do 
anything!

14
00:04:46,790 --> 00:04:49,750
Gimme your hand! 
Let go of the drill!

15
00:04:57,550 --> 00:05:01,010
Forget it, Jack! 
It's too late!

16
00:05:13,310 --> 00:05:15,310
You're not 
gonna make it!

17
00:05:24,490 --> 00:05:26,830
Jack!

18
00:05:37,420 --> 00:05:40,130
Jack! 
Gimme your hand!

19
00:05:40,210 --> 00:05:43,180
[ Yelling ]

20
00:05:43,260 --> 00:05:45,180
I got you!

21
00:05:45,220 --> 00:05:48,760
[ All Shouting ]

22
00:05:48,850 --> 00:05:52,180
What were you thinking? 
[ Sighs ]

23
00:05:52,230 --> 00:05:56,810
- What's happening? 
- The whole damn shelf 
is breaking off!

24
00:05:56,860 --> 00:05:59,360
That's 
what's happening!

25
00:06:02,940 --> 00:06:05,700
[ Cracking, Rumbling Continue ]

26
00:06:17,000 --> 00:06:19,290
[ Jack ] 
What we have found 
locked in these ice cores...

27
00:06:19,380 --> 00:06:21,590
is evidence of a cataclysmic 
climate shift,

28
00:06:21,670 --> 00:06:24,130
which occurred around 
10,000 years ago.

29
00:06:24,220 --> 00:06:28,510
The concentration of these 
natural greenhouse gases 
in the ice cores...

30
00:06:28,600 --> 00:06:31,850
indicates that runaway warming 
pushed the planet 
into an ice age...

31
00:06:31,890 --> 00:06:34,230
which lasted two centuries.

32
00:06:34,270 --> 00:06:37,650
[ Speaking 
Foreign Language ] 
I'm confused.

33
00:06:37,730 --> 00:06:42,110
I thought you were talking 
about global warming, 
not an ice age.

34
00:06:42,190 --> 00:06:46,530
Yes, it is a paradox, 
but global warming can trigger 
a cooling trend.

35
00:06:46,570 --> 00:06:48,660
Let me explain.

36
00:06:48,740 --> 00:06:53,540
The northern hemisphere 
owes its temperate climate 
to the North Atlantic current.

37
00:06:53,580 --> 00:06:57,830
Heat from the sun arrives 
at the equator and is 
carried north by the ocean.

38
00:06:57,920 --> 00:07:03,710
But global warming 
is melting the polar ice caps 
and disrupting this flow.

39
00:07:03,760 --> 00:07:05,800
Eventually, 
it will shut down.

40
00:07:05,880 --> 00:07:09,510
And when that occurs, 
there goes 
our warm climate.

41
00:07:09,590 --> 00:07:14,310
Excuse me. 
When do you think this 
could happen, Professor?

42
00:07:14,390 --> 00:07:16,230
When? 
I don't know.

43
00:07:16,270 --> 00:07:18,230
Maybe in a hundred years. 
Maybe in a thousand.

44
00:07:18,270 --> 00:07:21,150
But what I do know is 
that if we do not act soon,

45
00:07:21,230 --> 00:07:25,110
it is our children 
and our grandchildren 
who will have to pay the price.

46
00:07:26,820 --> 00:07:29,780
And who's going to pay 
the price of the Kyoto Accord?

47
00:07:29,870 --> 00:07:33,910
It would cost 
the world's economy hundreds 
of billions of dollars.

48
00:07:33,950 --> 00:07:36,410
With all due respect, 
Mr. Vice President,

49
00:07:36,450 --> 00:07:39,500
the cost of doing nothing 
could be even higher.

50
00:07:39,580 --> 00:07:42,170
Our climate is fragile.

51
00:07:42,250 --> 00:07:45,130
At the rate we're burning 
fossil fuels and polluting 
the environment,

52
00:07:45,170 --> 00:07:47,720
the ice caps 
will soon disappear.

53
00:07:47,800 --> 00:07:49,890
Professor, um, Hall,

54
00:07:51,340 --> 00:07:54,600
our economy is every bit 
as fragile as the environment.

55
00:07:54,640 --> 00:07:58,810
Perhaps you should keep that 
in mind before making 
sensationalist claims.

56
00:07:58,850 --> 00:08:02,400
Well, the last chunk of ice 
that broke off...

57
00:08:02,480 --> 00:08:04,820
was about the size 
of the state of Rhode Island.

58
00:08:04,900 --> 00:08:07,690
Some people might call that 
pretty sensational.

59
00:08:07,780 --> 00:08:09,910
[ Men Chuckling ]

60
00:08:11,200 --> 00:08:13,330
[ People Shouting ] 
Stop global warming!

61
00:08:13,410 --> 00:08:16,830
[ Man ] 
I'm here at the Global Warming 
Conference in New Delhi,

62
00:08:16,870 --> 00:08:19,120
where, if you can believe 
your eyes, it's snowing.

63
00:08:19,160 --> 00:08:21,420
The coldest weather 
on record...

64
00:08:21,500 --> 00:08:24,670
has thrown the city 
into chaos with numbers 
of homeless people--

65
00:08:24,750 --> 00:08:27,670
Taxi! Taxi!

66
00:08:27,760 --> 00:08:31,640
I enjoyed 
your testimony, Professor. 
It was very spirited.

67
00:08:31,680 --> 00:08:35,260
Oh, thank you. 
It's what we're here for, 
right? Put on a good show?

68
00:08:35,350 --> 00:08:38,730
Quite. Uh, 
I was wondering if 
I could talk to you...

69
00:08:38,810 --> 00:08:41,520
about your theory 
on abrupt climate shift.

70
00:08:41,560 --> 00:08:43,690
The name's Rapson. 
Terry Rapson.

71
00:08:43,770 --> 00:08:47,070
Professor Rapson? 
Of the Hedland Center? 
That's me.

72
00:08:47,150 --> 00:08:50,030
I've read your work 
on ocean currents.

73
00:08:50,110 --> 00:08:52,490
What do you say 
to a spot of tea? 
Absolutely.

74
00:08:52,530 --> 00:08:54,620
If we can hail a cab. 
Oh.

75
00:08:54,700 --> 00:08:57,620
[ Loud Whistle ]

76
00:08:57,660 --> 00:09:00,040
Over here!

77
00:09:00,120 --> 00:09:03,080
[ Thunder Rumbling ] 
[ Bell Clanging ]

78
00:09:17,470 --> 00:09:19,600
[ Man ] Welcome back 
to Glasgow, Scotland, 
[ Beeping ]

79
00:09:19,600 --> 00:09:21,310
We return 
to the action 63 minutes 
into the second half...

80
00:09:30,190 --> 00:09:34,030
Dennis, NOMAD buoy 4311 
is showing a temperature drop...

81
00:09:34,070 --> 00:09:36,660
of 13 degrees.

82
00:09:36,700 --> 00:09:40,160
- Hmm? Where is 4311? 
- Well, it's, uh,

83
00:09:43,830 --> 00:09:49,050
- Georges Bank. 
- Oh, it's rough seas out there. 
Must've knocked it about.

84
00:09:51,050 --> 00:09:54,720
[ Sheep Bleating ] 
[ Gulls Squawking ]

85
00:09:57,720 --> 00:09:59,720
[ Television Chatter Continues ]

86
00:09:59,720 --> 00:10:00,470
[ Thunderclap ] 
[ Announcement In Japanese ]

87
00:10:00,560 --> 00:10:02,560
[ Announcement Continues ]

88
00:10:11,360 --> 00:10:14,860
- [ Sirens Chirping ] 
- [ Speaking Japanese ]

89
00:10:16,490 --> 00:10:20,080
[ Cell Phone Ringing ] 
[ Speaking Japanese ]

90
00:10:21,540 --> 00:10:25,040
[ Woman On Phone 
Speaking Japanese ] 
[ Speaking Japanese ]

91
00:10:27,710 --> 00:10:30,340
[ Woman Continues ]

92
00:10:45,020 --> 00:10:46,980
[ People Screaming ]

93
00:11:10,090 --> 00:11:15,470
[ Woman Continues ]

94
00:11:22,010 --> 00:11:24,430
[ Man ] 
Parker, this is Houston.

95
00:11:24,520 --> 00:11:27,440
We're seeing 
some bad weather 
over Canaveral.

96
00:11:27,520 --> 00:11:29,600
Doesn't look like 
you're coming back 
this week.

97
00:11:29,690 --> 00:11:31,690
[ Chuckles ] 
Your wife's gonna 
give me an earful.

98
00:11:31,770 --> 00:11:34,360
[ Chuckles ] 
Roger that.

99
00:11:34,400 --> 00:11:36,570
[ Man #2 ] 
Hey, come take a look 
at this storm system.

100
00:11:36,650 --> 00:11:38,530
It's enormous.

101
00:11:50,630 --> 00:11:53,130
[ Thunder Rumbling ]

102
00:11:55,630 --> 00:11:57,510
[ Thunderclap ]

103
00:12:01,260 --> 00:12:03,470
I can't believe 
I'm spending two weeks 
alone with my mum.

104
00:12:03,560 --> 00:12:06,810
Be patient with her, all right? 
She's been looking forward 
to this holiday for months.

105
00:12:06,890 --> 00:12:08,810
I know. 
[ Both Chuckle ]

106
00:12:08,890 --> 00:12:13,230
I love you. 
I love you too.

107
00:12:15,070 --> 00:12:19,450
Hey. Hey, hello. 
Bye-bye.

108
00:12:19,490 --> 00:12:22,320
[ Man ] The cause 
of this extreme weather 
remains a mystery,

109
00:12:21,490 --> 00:12:25,990
although some meteorologists 
believe sunspots are to blame.

110
00:12:26,040 --> 00:12:28,040
This is very odd.

111
00:12:28,120 --> 00:12:32,170
There's a buoy here 
registering a 13 degree drop 
in ocean temperature.

112
00:12:32,250 --> 00:12:34,250
Oh, yeah. That's right.

113
00:12:34,290 --> 00:12:36,670
That buoy malfunctioned 
the other day.

114
00:12:36,760 --> 00:12:39,670
I'll put a call in, 
see if there are any ships 
near Georges Bank to get it.

115
00:12:39,720 --> 00:12:42,930
This buoy isn't 
in Georges Bank. 
It's just off Greenland.

116
00:12:43,010 --> 00:12:45,180
[ Man ] 
What?

117
00:12:46,560 --> 00:12:48,890
What are the odds 
of two buoys failing?

118
00:12:48,980 --> 00:12:50,810
Remote.

119
00:12:56,150 --> 00:12:59,190
Make that three.

120
00:13:17,130 --> 00:13:19,510
[ Barking ]

121
00:13:22,680 --> 00:13:25,050
- [ Roaring ] 
- What's got into them?

122
00:13:25,140 --> 00:13:29,520
[ Animals Howling, Calling ] 
I have no idea. 
They're all worked up today.

123
00:13:29,560 --> 00:13:30,640
[ Howling ]

124
00:13:35,400 --> 00:13:37,190
[ Phone Ringing ]

125
00:13:43,110 --> 00:13:48,080
Who is it? 
Terry Rapson here. Sorry 
to call you so early.

126
00:13:48,160 --> 00:13:50,290
No, Professor. 
That's all right. 
What is it?

127
00:13:50,370 --> 00:13:53,750
Well, we've found 
something extraordinary--

128
00:13:53,830 --> 00:13:56,380
extraordinary 
and disturbing, 
that is.

129
00:13:56,420 --> 00:13:58,550
You recall what you said 
in New Delhi...

130
00:13:58,590 --> 00:14:02,260
about how 
polar melting might disrupt 
the North Atlantic current?

131
00:14:02,300 --> 00:14:04,130
Yes.

132
00:14:04,220 --> 00:14:06,010
Well,

133
00:14:06,090 --> 00:14:08,220
I think it's happening.

134
00:14:11,930 --> 00:14:14,640
What do you mean? 
One of our 
NOMAD buoys...

135
00:14:14,730 --> 00:14:18,730
registered a 13 degree drop 
in surface temperature 
the other day.

136
00:14:18,820 --> 00:14:21,110
I sent you 
an e-mail. 
Hold on.

137
00:14:23,530 --> 00:14:26,110
At first we thought 
it was a malfunction,

138
00:14:26,160 --> 00:14:30,330
but there are four more 
across the North Atlantic 
showing the same thing.

139
00:14:30,410 --> 00:14:32,790
[ Jack ] 
This is unbelievable.

140
00:14:32,830 --> 00:14:35,620
You predicted 
it would happen.

141
00:14:35,710 --> 00:14:38,880
Yes, but not 
in our lifetime. 
This is too fast.

142
00:14:38,960 --> 00:14:44,340
There are no forecast models 
remotely capable 
of plotting this scenario,

143
00:14:44,420 --> 00:14:46,300
except yours.

144
00:14:46,380 --> 00:14:49,470
My model is a reconstruction 
of a prehistoric climate shift.

145
00:14:49,550 --> 00:14:54,100
It's not a forecast model. 
It's the closest thing 
we have.

146
00:14:54,180 --> 00:14:57,980
Nothing like this 
has every happened before.

147
00:14:58,020 --> 00:15:01,150
At least not in 
the last 10,000 years.

148
00:15:27,180 --> 00:15:28,340
[ Ringing ]

149
00:15:28,430 --> 00:15:31,180
Shouldn't you be monitoring 
the weather or something?

150
00:15:31,260 --> 00:15:33,770
This is L.A. 
What weather?

151
00:15:36,060 --> 00:15:38,980
[ Motor Running, Faint ] 
Wait. 
What's that noise?

152
00:15:39,020 --> 00:15:41,860
What noise?

153
00:15:41,940 --> 00:15:46,070
[ Motor Running, Louder ]

154
00:15:46,150 --> 00:15:48,450
[ Motor Stops ] 
[ Phone Ringing ]

155
00:15:50,030 --> 00:15:54,200
[ Sighs ] 
Honey, I'll be 
right back.

156
00:15:56,040 --> 00:15:57,100
[ Woman ] 
The coast guard has 
closed the beaches...

157
00:15:58,040 --> 00:16:00,380
as waves have been too big 
for even the most experienced 
of surfers.

158
00:16:00,420 --> 00:16:02,670
L.A. Weather Center. 
It's Tommy! 
I'm down at the beach!

159
00:16:02,710 --> 00:16:04,710
I'm in the middle 
of something. 
What do you need?

160
00:16:04,800 --> 00:16:07,720
There-- Ooh! Uh.

161
00:16:07,760 --> 00:16:10,760
There's-- There's hail 
the size of golf balls 
coming down here!

162
00:16:10,840 --> 00:16:12,850
[ Tommy Continues, Indistinct ] 
[ Man ] Low pressure system 
moving along...

163
00:16:12,890 --> 00:16:16,270
the California coastline 
is creating a cyclonic system 
across the L.A. basin.

164
00:16:16,350 --> 00:16:19,480
[ Phone Ringing ]

165
00:16:23,520 --> 00:16:26,110
Yeah. Hello. 
Hey, boss, turn on 
the Weather Channel.

166
00:16:26,190 --> 00:16:28,110
I think we have to issue 
a tornado warning.

167
00:16:28,190 --> 00:16:30,820
What are 
you talking about?

168
00:16:30,910 --> 00:16:32,870
[ Woman ] 
Palmdale and Lancaster 
are reporting...

169
00:16:32,910 --> 00:16:35,370
wind speeds in excess 
of 70 miles per hour, 
Hold on a second.

170
00:16:35,450 --> 00:16:37,620
conditions that are 
highly unusual for California.

171
00:17:00,270 --> 00:17:03,650
[ Woman ] 
We have live coverage now 
from our Fox 11 chopper.

172
00:17:03,690 --> 00:17:06,270
Are you there, Bart? 
Uh, yes, I'm here.

173
00:17:06,360 --> 00:17:09,440
These tornadoes are forming 
so fast-- What?

174
00:17:09,530 --> 00:17:12,700
[ Bart ] 
Oh! Oh, my God! 
Lisa--

175
00:17:12,740 --> 00:17:16,410
Are you getting this on camera? 
This tornado just came 
and erased the Hollywood sign.

176
00:17:16,490 --> 00:17:20,120
The Hollywood sign is gone. 
It's just shredded.

177
00:17:20,200 --> 00:17:23,670
[ Wind Roaring ] 
[ People Screaming ]

178
00:17:32,090 --> 00:17:34,390
[ Lisa ] 
Bart, what can you see? 
Is anyone hurt?

179
00:17:34,470 --> 00:17:37,180
[ Bart ] 
I wouldn't be surprised. There 
is so much damage down there.

180
00:17:37,220 --> 00:17:40,060
And there are people 
down there taking pictures!

181
00:17:40,140 --> 00:17:42,350
Hey! What the hell 
are you guys doing!

182
00:17:42,390 --> 00:17:45,980
Go for cover! 
You can't stay here! 
Get outta here!

183
00:17:48,610 --> 00:17:53,700
What you're seeing are two 
actual tornadoes striking Los 
Angeles International Airport.

184
00:17:53,740 --> 00:17:56,910
Wait. Wait. It looks like 
they've joined and formed 
one large tornado.

185
00:17:56,990 --> 00:18:00,370
Tommy! 
Oh, my God!

186
00:18:02,410 --> 00:18:05,290
[ Man ] 
Urge all of our viewers to stay 
away from the area.

187
00:18:05,370 --> 00:18:10,250
- Jeff, where are you? 
- I'm on, uh, Yucca and Vine. 
I'm on my way in now.

188
00:18:10,340 --> 00:18:12,260
Jeff, you're on TV! 
You're right 
in the middle of it!

189
00:18:12,340 --> 00:18:14,430
God. Oh, my God!

190
00:18:21,930 --> 00:18:24,100
You gotta get 
out of there, man!

191
00:18:24,190 --> 00:18:26,270
Oh.

192
00:18:28,770 --> 00:18:30,650
[ Bart ] 
That bus just got dropped 
on top of that Porsche!

193
00:18:30,730 --> 00:18:34,570
[ Line Busy Tone ] 
Oh, my God. I hope 
no one was in that car!

194
00:18:34,610 --> 00:18:36,610
[ Woman ] 
And for our national audience 
just joining us now,

195
00:18:36,660 --> 00:18:38,780
we are going live 
to downtown Los Angeles 
right now.

196
00:18:38,870 --> 00:18:42,100
- Tommy? 
- If you look over there 
behind me, that's a tornado.

197
00:18:43,080 --> 00:18:45,620
Yes! A twister 
in Los Angeles.

198
00:18:45,710 --> 00:18:50,040
It's one of the many 
tornadoes that are 
destroying our city.

199
00:18:50,130 --> 00:18:53,590
There's another one! 
That's the Los Angeles 
skyline!

200
00:18:53,630 --> 00:18:57,180
It's unbelievable! 
It's huge! I've never 
seen anything like that!

201
00:18:58,430 --> 00:19:00,970
[ Gasping ]

202
00:19:08,810 --> 00:19:10,860
What's happening?

203
00:19:27,920 --> 00:19:31,290
[ Rumbling Stops ]

204
00:19:45,020 --> 00:19:48,230
[ Wind Howling ]

205
00:19:55,400 --> 00:19:58,320
[ Car Alarms Blaring ]

206
00:20:17,590 --> 00:20:19,840
[ Chattering ]

207
00:20:21,260 --> 00:20:23,550
All right. All right. 
Listen up, everybody.

208
00:20:23,640 --> 00:20:27,770
Listen up, please. 
We've got a lot of work to do 
and we don't have much time.

209
00:20:27,810 --> 00:20:31,940
So, let's get started, 
please. Vorsteen?

210
00:20:32,020 --> 00:20:34,610
All our grid models 
are worthless.

211
00:20:34,650 --> 00:20:36,820
I don't think grid models 
are gonna be a lot of help here.

212
00:20:36,900 --> 00:20:40,610
The Canadians are reporting 
tremendous circulation 
moving down from the Arctic.

213
00:20:40,700 --> 00:20:44,030
In Siberia, there's 
a low pressure system 
unlike anything we've seen,

214
00:20:44,080 --> 00:20:46,910
and Australia just saw 
the strongest typhoon 
ever recorded.

215
00:20:46,990 --> 00:20:49,100
[ Man ] 
Hang on. Are you saying that 
these things are interconnected?

216
00:20:50,080 --> 00:20:51,870
[ Booker ] 
We have to consider 
the possibility.

217
00:20:51,920 --> 00:20:54,880
The only force strong enough 
to affect global weather 
is the sun.

218
00:20:54,920 --> 00:20:59,760
- What's NASA have to say? 
- We've already checked. 
Solar output is normal.

219
00:20:59,840 --> 00:21:04,890
What about 
the North Atlantic current? 
[ Vorsteen ] What about it?

220
00:21:04,970 --> 00:21:07,970
I got a call last night 
from Professor Rapson 
at the Hedland Center.

221
00:21:08,930 --> 00:21:10,770
He thinks the current 
has changed.

222
00:21:10,850 --> 00:21:12,900
[ Murmuring ]

223
00:21:12,980 --> 00:21:15,270
Come on, Jack. 
How could that be?

224
00:21:15,360 --> 00:21:18,190
The current depends upon 
a delicate balance of salt 
and fresh water.

225
00:21:18,230 --> 00:21:21,650
- We all know that. 
- Yes.

226
00:21:21,740 --> 00:21:23,740
But no one has 
taken into account...

227
00:21:23,820 --> 00:21:28,200
how much fresh water 
has been dumped into the ocean 
because of melting polar ice.

228
00:21:28,240 --> 00:21:30,910
I think we've hit 
a critical desalinization point.

229
00:21:30,100 --> 00:21:33,170
[ Murmuring ]

230
00:21:34,790 --> 00:21:37,040
It would explain 
what's driving 
this extreme weather.

231
00:21:37,090 --> 00:21:40,010
Hedland had some pretty 
convincing data.

232
00:21:40,050 --> 00:21:44,010
They've asked me to feed it into 
my paleoclimate model to track 
the next set of events.

233
00:21:44,090 --> 00:21:47,890
Hold on, Jack. Are you 
suggesting these weather 
anomalies are gonna continue?

234
00:21:47,930 --> 00:21:50,980
Not just continue-- 
get worse.

235
00:21:55,100 --> 00:21:57,610
I think we're on the verge 
of a major climate shift.

236
00:21:57,690 --> 00:22:00,190
[ Murmuring ]

237
00:22:02,610 --> 00:22:05,780
Tom! 
What are you gonna tell 
the administration?

238
00:22:05,870 --> 00:22:07,700
What do you expect me 
to tell them?

239
00:22:07,780 --> 00:22:10,580
The government has 
to start making long-term 
preparations now.

240
00:22:10,660 --> 00:22:13,540
Jack, all you have is a theory. 
Then give me the mainframe 
and let me prove it!

241
00:22:13,620 --> 00:22:16,630
No.

242
00:22:17,790 --> 00:22:19,800
You have 48 hours.

243
00:22:19,840 --> 00:22:21,840
[ Sighs ] 
Professor Hall.

244
00:22:21,920 --> 00:22:24,300
Yes? 
I think your theory 
may be correct.

245
00:22:24,380 --> 00:22:25,970
Walk with me.

246
00:22:26,010 --> 00:22:29,890
Just a few weeks ago, 
I monitored the strongest 
hurricane on record.

247
00:22:29,970 --> 00:22:32,600
The hail, the tornadoes-- 
it all fits.

248
00:22:32,640 --> 00:22:35,230
Can the model 
you're working on 
factor in storm scenarios?

249
00:22:35,310 --> 00:22:38,810
Oh, we haven't had 
the time. 
Maybe I can help.

250
00:22:38,900 --> 00:22:40,480
Welcome aboard. 
Thanks.

251
00:22:46,660 --> 00:22:50,030
Jack. Jack. 
We got the results.

252
00:22:58,790 --> 00:23:01,090
Six to eight months. 
That can't be.

253
00:23:01,130 --> 00:23:04,880
That time scale 
isn't in months. 
It's in weeks.

254
00:23:08,550 --> 00:23:10,390
So far, the terrible weather 
hasn't hit the D.C. area,

255
00:23:10,470 --> 00:23:13,180
but local residents 
aren't taking any chances,

256
00:23:13,220 --> 00:23:15,850
as people stock up 
for what is already 
being billed...

257
00:23:15,890 --> 00:23:18,560
as the worst storm season 
on record.

258
00:23:18,650 --> 00:23:21,070
You better be sure 
about this, Jack. 
My ass is on the line.

259
00:23:21,150 --> 00:23:24,240
You saw the model. 
And I hope to God 
it's wrong.

260
00:23:24,320 --> 00:23:26,700
Oh. 
Mr. Vice President. 
Tom.

261
00:23:26,740 --> 00:23:29,450
You know 
Professor Hall. 
Yes, we've met.

262
00:23:29,530 --> 00:23:33,040
Uh, Professor Hall has some 
new information I think 
you should take a look at.

263
00:23:33,080 --> 00:23:35,870
We just got these results 
from our simulation model.

264
00:23:35,960 --> 00:23:38,040
They explain what's causing 
the severe weather.

265
00:23:38,120 --> 00:23:41,710
I'll look at this later. 
I have a meeting with 
the director of FEMA now.

266
00:23:41,750 --> 00:23:44,550
This is very urgent, sir. 
Our climate 
is changing violently.

267
00:23:44,630 --> 00:23:47,220
It's going to happen 
over the next six 
to eight weeks.

268
00:23:47,300 --> 00:23:50,050
I thought you said this 
wouldn't happen for another 
hundred years or so.

269
00:23:50,140 --> 00:23:52,720
I was wrong. 
Well, suppose 
you're wrong this time.

270
00:23:52,760 --> 00:23:56,730
I wish that I were, but I'm sure 
that you're aware of what's 
happening all around the world.

271
00:23:56,770 --> 00:24:00,270
We're making all the necessary 
preparations for this storm. 
What more do you expect?

272
00:24:00,350 --> 00:24:04,280
You have to start 
thinking about large scale 
evacuations right now,

273
00:24:04,360 --> 00:24:06,320
especially in 
the northern states.

274
00:24:06,400 --> 00:24:08,450
Evacuations? 
Yes.

275
00:24:08,530 --> 00:24:12,370
Have you lost 
your mind, Hall? 
I have to go.

276
00:24:12,410 --> 00:24:17,370
Mr. Vice President, 
if we don't act now, 
it's going to be too late.

277
00:24:21,250 --> 00:24:23,090
Come on, Jack.

278
00:24:26,010 --> 00:24:29,340
[ Man ] 
It's been 24 hours now since 
the snow started falling...

279
00:24:29,380 --> 00:24:31,300
across the British Isles 
and over northern Europe,

280
00:24:31,390 --> 00:24:33,510
and it shows no signs 
of letting up.

281
00:24:33,550 --> 00:24:36,180
No. No, no, no. 
You gotta stop worrying. 
No.

282
00:24:36,220 --> 00:24:40,270
It's fine. We got plenty 
of supplies with us. 
We're just snowed in.

283
00:24:40,350 --> 00:24:43,360
Yeah. No, it's all right. 
It's all right.

284
00:24:43,400 --> 00:24:45,360
You stay 
where you are, okay? 
I'll be fine.

285
00:24:45,400 --> 00:24:49,240
Yeah, I love you too. 
Okay. Bye. 
[ Sighs ]

286
00:24:49,280 --> 00:24:53,820
- Hey. How's Jeanette? 
- Oh, fine.

287
00:24:53,910 --> 00:24:58,910
Ferry just landed. 
Must be nice in Spain. 
I wish I was there.

288
00:24:58,100 --> 00:25:01,580
[ TV: Reporter ] 
The search and rescue team has 
been deployed by helicopter...

289
00:25:01,670 --> 00:25:04,540
to airlift 
the royal family to safety.

290
00:25:04,590 --> 00:25:06,710
You think 
they'll come get us, 
since we got snowed in?

291
00:25:06,750 --> 00:25:09,170
Not likely.

292
00:25:09,260 --> 00:25:11,220
Luckily, 
we got our own genny,

293
00:25:11,260 --> 00:25:13,760
enough tea and biscuits 
to sink a ship.

294
00:25:13,800 --> 00:25:17,350
Oh, we'll be fine. 
As long as the loo 
doesn't back up again.

295
00:25:17,430 --> 00:25:19,430
[ All Chuckle ]

296
00:25:22,270 --> 00:25:26,570
In Ireland, those remaining 
in Belfast and Banbridge 
have been urged to evacuate...

297
00:25:26,610 --> 00:25:29,780
to Dublin as soon 
as possible.

298
00:25:29,860 --> 00:25:33,950
The north continues 
to receive the worst 
of the storm--

299
00:25:33,990 --> 00:25:35,950
[ Helicopter Approaching ]

300
00:25:38,490 --> 00:25:41,790
[ Man ] 
We've got zero visibility. 
Maintain heading and speed.

301
00:25:41,830 --> 00:25:44,170
- [ Man #2 ] 
What's our heading? 
- Zero, three, five, sir.

302
00:25:44,250 --> 00:25:47,460
Approximately 40 kilometers 
from Balmoral Castle.

303
00:25:47,500 --> 00:25:49,630
[ Man ] 
We've lost visual contact 
with you.

304
00:26:13,650 --> 00:26:15,660
This gauge here 
can't be right.

305
00:26:15,700 --> 00:26:17,660
[ Man #3 ] 
Wind speed has dropped to zero.

306
00:26:17,740 --> 00:26:20,240
We have vertical walls 
of cirrus cloud formations.

307
00:26:20,330 --> 00:26:21,830
[ Man ] 
What the hell 
is going on?

308
00:26:21,870 --> 00:26:23,830
Bloody fuel lines 
are starting to freeze!

309
00:26:28,340 --> 00:26:30,840
[ Men Shouting ]

310
00:26:35,050 --> 00:26:38,970
Port engine pressure's dropping! 
The rotor R.P.M. is dropping!

311
00:26:39,050 --> 00:26:41,100
We've got a flame-out 
on the starboard side as well!

312
00:26:45,690 --> 00:26:47,860
Prepare 
for crash landing!

313
00:26:49,360 --> 00:26:52,360
Select emergency fuel!

314
00:26:52,400 --> 00:26:55,030
Come on, 
you bastard!

315
00:26:55,110 --> 00:26:57,490
Come on!

316
00:27:10,790 --> 00:27:13,170
[ Breathing Heavily ]

317
00:27:20,260 --> 00:27:23,180
[ Rapson ] 
What I'm about to tell you 
is supposed to be confidential.

318
00:27:23,220 --> 00:27:27,060
Several hours ago, 
three helicopters went down 
over Scotland.

319
00:27:27,140 --> 00:27:30,650
They crashed because the fuel 
in their lines froze.

320
00:27:30,730 --> 00:27:32,610
At what temperature does--

321
00:27:32,690 --> 00:27:37,860
[ Rapson On Speaker ] 
Negative 150 degrees Fahrenheit. 
We had to look it up.

322
00:27:37,910 --> 00:27:40,160
The temperature dropped 
phenomenally fast.

323
00:27:40,240 --> 00:27:43,580
On the ground, people froze 
before they could 
get out of their cars.

324
00:27:43,620 --> 00:27:47,710
- Get me a satellite picture 
of Scotland two hours ago. 
- Yeah.

325
00:27:47,750 --> 00:27:52,170
We've got mountains of data 
but nowhere near enough 
computer power to analyze it.

326
00:27:52,250 --> 00:27:55,590
- Can you help us? 
- Send us what you've got, 
and we'll do our best.

327
00:27:55,670 --> 00:27:59,260
[ Rapson ] 
Thanks, Jack. Bye for now.

328
00:27:59,300 --> 00:28:02,640
This is Scotland at the time 
the temperature dropped.

329
00:28:05,100 --> 00:28:07,890
[ Frank ] 
This thing looks 
just like a hurricane.

330
00:28:07,940 --> 00:28:10,770
[ Janet ] 
Only hurricanes don't form 
over land.

331
00:28:11,980 --> 00:28:14,360
It's a mob scene here 
at Grand Central Station.

332
00:28:14,440 --> 00:28:16,650
Over half the platforms 
are flooded,

333
00:28:16,740 --> 00:28:19,570
and service has been 
suspended on all trains.

334
00:28:19,660 --> 00:28:24,160
With planes still grounded 
and trains now out of service, 
that's bad news--

335
00:28:24,240 --> 00:28:27,870
Victor's coming to pick me up. 
Do you guys want a ride 
to the train station?

336
00:28:27,960 --> 00:28:31,130
Stranded 
since this morning. 
Not anymore.

337
00:28:31,170 --> 00:28:35,590
In Nova Scotia earlier, 
the ocean rose by 25 feet 
in a matter of seconds.

338
00:28:35,670 --> 00:28:39,970
What we have feared 
for the past few days 
has indeed happened.

339
00:28:40,010 --> 00:28:42,180
The cold front moving down 
from the Arctic...

340
00:28:42,260 --> 00:28:45,100
has created an enormous 
storm system in Canada,

341
00:28:45,180 --> 00:28:48,520
which, incredible as it sounds, 
looks like a tropical hurricane.

342
00:28:48,600 --> 00:28:52,100
I gotta go pick up 
my little brother. 
Do you guys want a ride?

343
00:28:52,190 --> 00:28:55,480
Where is he? 
He's in a boarding school 
in Philadelphia.

344
00:28:55,520 --> 00:29:00,360
If this system moves south, 
we could see a wind-driven 
storm surge...

345
00:29:00,450 --> 00:29:03,700
that could threaten 
the entire Eastern Seaboard.

346
00:29:03,780 --> 00:29:07,870
Okay. Bye. Victor's stuck 
in traffic on Fifth Avenue.

347
00:29:07,950 --> 00:29:10,100
It'll be easier 
to head out of town 
if we meet him over there.

348
00:29:11,040 --> 00:29:14,130
All right. 
You mean walk? 
No, not in this.

349
00:29:15,420 --> 00:29:17,920
[ Thunder Rumbling ]

350
00:29:18,010 --> 00:29:20,380
We should take 
the stairs.

351
00:29:20,470 --> 00:29:23,090
We're on the top floor.

352
00:29:25,350 --> 00:29:27,180
I guess we're walkin'.

353
00:29:29,640 --> 00:29:31,850
[ Thunderclap ]

354
00:29:31,940 --> 00:29:35,230
[ Horns Honking, 
People Shouting ]

355
00:29:39,570 --> 00:29:43,910
Maybe we should just stay here. 
I think the young lady 
is right, sir.

356
00:29:43,990 --> 00:29:46,910
No. We need to get home.

357
00:29:46,950 --> 00:29:50,330
Come on! 
[ Shouting Continues ]

358
00:29:59,630 --> 00:30:02,840
[ Goats Bleating ]

359
00:30:05,390 --> 00:30:07,680
[ Growls ]

360
00:30:11,600 --> 00:30:13,770
Hey, Cesar, come here.

361
00:30:13,810 --> 00:30:15,900
What are you doing?

362
00:30:20,650 --> 00:30:23,530
The wolves! 
They're gone!

363
00:30:23,610 --> 00:30:26,240
[ Man On News ] 
Just to give you an idea 
of the situation,

364
00:30:26,280 --> 00:30:29,410
which seems to be becoming 
worse with each passing minute,

365
00:30:29,450 --> 00:30:33,460
at the moment, we have flooding 
in most parts of the island.

366
00:30:33,540 --> 00:30:36,500
We've got traffic snarl-ups 
because the electricity 
is now out...

367
00:30:36,580 --> 00:30:39,460
to almost every part 
of Manhattan.

368
00:30:39,550 --> 00:30:43,050
No traffic signals, 
car accidents-- at least 200--

369
00:30:43,130 --> 00:30:47,260
and Lower Manhattan, we've been 
told, is virtually inaccessible.

370
00:30:47,340 --> 00:30:51,890
[ Man ] 
No one is to be on the streets. 
Please go inside.

371
00:30:51,930 --> 00:30:55,350
[ Excited Chattering ] 
Sir, just calm down.

372
00:30:57,190 --> 00:30:58,610
Public library's right up here. 
Make your way up there. 
You can stay warm and dry.

373
00:31:00,070 --> 00:31:02,650
Come on, guys. 
This way.

374
00:31:02,730 --> 00:31:05,070
Excuse me. 
Your bad.

375
00:31:05,150 --> 00:31:08,120
Goddamn $1,500 
waterproof raincoat!

376
00:31:08,160 --> 00:31:10,030
Please shut up, man.

377
00:31:10,120 --> 00:31:12,950
There must be rats 
everywhere. 
That's 'cause it's New York.

378
00:31:12,100 --> 00:31:17,040
Hey! Hey! 
Open up, big poppa!

379
00:31:17,120 --> 00:31:20,790
It's out of service. 
It's out of service!

380
00:31:20,880 --> 00:31:23,760
No, no. I'll give you 
$100 to put it in service. 
You don't have to do that.

381
00:31:23,840 --> 00:31:27,260
No, no. Really, $200. 
I won't have it. 
I won't have it.

382
00:31:27,340 --> 00:31:30,140
Oh, God, I love buses. 
This is just so much fun.

383
00:31:30,180 --> 00:31:32,680
[ Shouting ] 
Hello?

384
00:31:32,760 --> 00:31:35,390
I can't reach my driver. 
I lost the signal.

385
00:31:35,480 --> 00:31:38,150
This is insane. 
We should just go back 
to your apartment.

386
00:31:38,190 --> 00:31:40,150
[ Brian ] 
Yeah. I vote for that.

387
00:31:40,190 --> 00:31:42,820
What? Are you kidding? 
We have to get higher!

388
00:31:42,860 --> 00:31:45,690
Come on. 
Up to the library.

389
00:31:45,690 --> 00:31:48,950
[ Thunderclap ]

390
00:32:10,470 --> 00:32:13,220
[ Shouting Continues ]

391
00:32:14,560 --> 00:32:16,850
- [ Child Screams ] 
- [ Thunderclap ]

392
00:32:34,620 --> 00:32:37,910
[ Man On News ] 
There is a wall of water 
coming towards New York City!

393
00:32:37,960 --> 00:32:39,830
Everybody--

394
00:32:41,170 --> 00:32:43,630
[ Shouting, Screaming ]

395
00:32:47,460 --> 00:32:50,380
- [ Woman Screams ] 
- [ Shouting Continues ]

396
00:33:25,840 --> 00:33:27,840
Oh, my--

397
00:33:33,930 --> 00:33:36,010
Laura!

398
00:33:40,930 --> 00:33:43,350
Laura!

399
00:33:43,440 --> 00:33:45,270
No! No! 
No, Sam! Sam, no!

400
00:33:45,360 --> 00:33:49,570
Laura! Laura! Laura! 
Come on! 
[ Gasps ]

401
00:33:49,610 --> 00:33:52,860
Come! Come on!

402
00:33:54,200 --> 00:33:56,990
- Sam! 
- Go!

403
00:34:15,260 --> 00:34:18,640
[ Dog Barking ]

404
00:34:33,700 --> 00:34:35,530
Here he is.

405
00:34:37,740 --> 00:34:41,580
Jack, were you able 
to re-create the thermal cycle?

406
00:34:41,660 --> 00:34:46,880
Yes. The storm's rotation 
is pulling super-cooled air 
down from the upper troposphere.

407
00:34:50,000 --> 00:34:54,010
But shouldn't the air warm up 
before it reaches ground level?

408
00:34:54,050 --> 00:34:58,550
It should, but it doesn't. 
The air's descending 
too rapidly.

409
00:34:58,640 --> 00:35:01,810
Is this 
an isolated incident?

410
00:35:04,230 --> 00:35:06,230
I'm afraid not.

411
00:35:06,270 --> 00:35:10,020
We've located two other 
supercells in addition 
to the one over Scotland.

412
00:35:10,070 --> 00:35:14,360
There's one over Northern Canada 
and another one over Siberia.

413
00:35:14,400 --> 00:35:17,950
And do we know 
their projected path?

414
00:35:18,030 --> 00:35:22,580
Yes. Our previous estimates 
of six to eight weeks 
weren't even close.

415
00:35:22,620 --> 00:35:26,250
This one storm is going 
to change the face 
of our planet.

416
00:35:26,330 --> 00:35:29,460
Here's a projection 
of 24 hours out.

417
00:35:34,420 --> 00:35:37,340
This is 48 hours out.

418
00:35:39,300 --> 00:35:41,600
And in seven to 10 days.

419
00:35:45,140 --> 00:35:49,440
When this storm is over, 
we'll be in a new ice age.

420
00:35:54,150 --> 00:35:56,070
My God.

421
00:36:14,460 --> 00:36:18,510
Professor, it's time 
you got out of there.

422
00:36:20,220 --> 00:36:23,640
I'm afraid that time 
has come and gone, my friend.

423
00:36:28,480 --> 00:36:30,940
What can we do?

424
00:36:34,070 --> 00:36:36,650
Save as many as you can.

425
00:36:36,690 --> 00:36:39,700
[ Feedback On Line ]

426
00:36:41,700 --> 00:36:44,120
[ Disconnect Signal ]

427
00:36:47,450 --> 00:36:50,420
[ Phone Ringing ]

428
00:36:51,670 --> 00:36:55,750
Jack. Something's 
happened in New York.

429
00:37:05,850 --> 00:37:09,060
No. The power is out. 
I've been in here all day.

430
00:37:09,140 --> 00:37:12,350
[ Officer ] 
Who needs help? 
Over here. Over here.

431
00:37:12,400 --> 00:37:14,860
[ Indistinct Shouting ] 
Hi.

432
00:37:14,900 --> 00:37:17,650
[ Man ] 
Hello. 
[ Dog Whimpering ]

433
00:37:18,940 --> 00:37:21,530
What?

434
00:37:21,610 --> 00:37:26,660
This is the last one. 
Enjoy it. Greedy.

435
00:37:26,700 --> 00:37:29,250
[ Operator ] 
All circuits are busy.

436
00:37:29,250 --> 00:37:31,040
[ Dialing Phone ]

437
00:37:31,080 --> 00:37:33,750
Did you reach 
your little brother yet?

438
00:37:33,830 --> 00:37:38,550
No. There's still no service. 
Damn cell phones.

439
00:37:41,930 --> 00:37:44,010
- Excuse me. 
- Yes?

440
00:37:44,050 --> 00:37:46,850
Are there any pay phones 
on the upper floors? 
No. No.

441
00:37:46,890 --> 00:37:49,680
But there are some 
on the mezzanine. 
Great.

442
00:37:49,720 --> 00:37:52,140
- But I think it's underwater. 
- Where are you going?

443
00:37:52,190 --> 00:37:56,110
The power's out. 
Older pay phones draw their 
power directly from the line.

444
00:37:56,190 --> 00:37:58,730
[ Woman On P.A., Indistinct ]

445
00:37:58,820 --> 00:38:02,530
Oh, God. I've been trying 
to reach Sam.

446
00:38:02,570 --> 00:38:06,780
So have I. 
I kept trying to call you, 
but I couldn't get through.

447
00:38:06,870 --> 00:38:09,240
It's been a madhouse 
around here. Come on.

448
00:38:14,420 --> 00:38:16,790
Are you sure about this?

449
00:38:19,710 --> 00:38:22,260
Ah. Oh.

450
00:38:24,590 --> 00:38:28,220
- [ Dial Tone ] 
- It works.

451
00:38:28,220 --> 00:38:32,770
Jack? Sam's on the phone. 
Line four.

452
00:38:32,810 --> 00:38:35,350
- Sam? 
- Dad!

453
00:38:35,440 --> 00:38:38,440
Where are you? 
Are you all right? 
We're at the public library.

454
00:38:38,520 --> 00:38:42,440
Sam, it's Mom. 
I'm so happy you're okay.

455
00:38:42,530 --> 00:38:47,240
Mom, can you call 
Laura and Brian's parents 
and tell them we're all right?

456
00:38:47,280 --> 00:38:49,530
Yes. Of course.

457
00:38:49,620 --> 00:38:53,330
- Sam, what's that noise? 
- Sam? Sam?

458
00:38:53,410 --> 00:38:58,460
[ Gasping ] 
What is going on out there, Dad?

459
00:38:58,540 --> 00:39:02,630
Sam? Sam, listen to me. 
Listen very carefully.

460
00:39:02,710 --> 00:39:05,340
Forget what I said 
about trying to head south.

461
00:39:05,430 --> 00:39:08,430
It's too late for that. 
The storm is just gonna 
get worse.

462
00:39:08,470 --> 00:39:12,600
It's gonna turn into 
a massive blizzard with an eye 
in the center, like a hurricane.

463
00:39:12,640 --> 00:39:15,350
- Uh-huh. 
- The air is gonna be so cold,

464
00:39:15,440 --> 00:39:18,060
you could freeze to death 
in seconds.

465
00:39:20,360 --> 00:39:23,650
- Sam? 
- What should we do?

466
00:39:23,740 --> 00:39:26,950
Listen to me, son. 
Do not go outside.

467
00:39:26,990 --> 00:39:30,740
Just burn whatever you can 
to stay warm and try 
to wait it out.

468
00:39:30,780 --> 00:39:34,160
I will come for you. 
Do you understand me? 
I will come for you.

469
00:39:34,200 --> 00:39:36,620
[ Shivering ]

470
00:39:36,710 --> 00:39:40,670
Sam! Sam, come back!

471
00:39:40,710 --> 00:39:43,880
[ Jack ] 
Sam? Sam, did you hear me?

472
00:39:43,960 --> 00:39:46,010
Did you hear me?

473
00:40:05,440 --> 00:40:08,740
[ Gasping ]

474
00:40:08,780 --> 00:40:11,240
[ Shivering, Panting ]

475
00:40:11,280 --> 00:40:14,120
[ Sobbing ] 
I thought you drowned.

476
00:40:19,120 --> 00:40:23,210
Let's find some 
dry clothes for you. 
Yeah.

477
00:40:28,760 --> 00:40:31,640
Where did you store 
the arctic gear?

478
00:40:31,720 --> 00:40:34,060
You can't make it 
to New York, Jack.

479
00:40:34,140 --> 00:40:36,980
I've walked that far 
before in the snow.

480
00:40:37,060 --> 00:40:41,600
This is not the same. 
Jack, this is not the same!

481
00:40:41,650 --> 00:40:43,730
Lucy, tell him.

482
00:40:45,320 --> 00:40:47,400
I have to do this.

483
00:40:49,650 --> 00:40:51,320
I know.

484
00:40:57,660 --> 00:41:00,210
Frank told me about Sam.

485
00:41:00,250 --> 00:41:02,580
I'm not gonna try 
to talk you out of going,

486
00:41:02,670 --> 00:41:05,460
but there's something 
I need for you to do first.

487
00:41:05,550 --> 00:41:08,260
You have to explain your results 
to the administration.

488
00:41:08,340 --> 00:41:10,430
I already tried that. 
I know this time 
will be different.

489
00:41:10,470 --> 00:41:13,300
You're gonna brief 
the president directly.

490
00:41:15,930 --> 00:41:17,890
[ Officer ] 
Is that the last of it?

491
00:41:17,930 --> 00:41:20,520
[ Laura ] 
Yeah. Pretty much. 
Okay.

492
00:41:20,600 --> 00:41:23,100
We also found this radio, 
but I don't think it works.

493
00:41:23,190 --> 00:41:25,900
Let me see it.

494
00:41:25,940 --> 00:41:29,280
[ Barking ]

495
00:41:30,990 --> 00:41:33,530
Buddha, be quiet!

496
00:41:33,620 --> 00:41:37,200
You ain't even supposed 
to be in here anyway.

497
00:41:37,290 --> 00:41:42,040
- [ Barking Continues ] 
- [ Loud Rumbling ]

498
00:41:47,300 --> 00:41:50,090
[ Rumbling Continues ]

499
00:42:20,700 --> 00:42:24,080
[ Barking Continues ]

500
00:42:31,840 --> 00:42:33,340
Come on, guys.

501
00:42:40,140 --> 00:42:42,350
Oh, my God.

502
00:42:51,780 --> 00:42:54,360
[ Glass Shattering ]

503
00:43:04,790 --> 00:43:07,630
The basic rule of storms 
is that they continue...

504
00:43:07,670 --> 00:43:10,750
until the imbalance 
that created them is corrected.

505
00:43:10,840 --> 00:43:14,510
In this case, we're talking 
about a global realignment.

506
00:43:14,590 --> 00:43:17,930
The superstorm will 
last seven to 10 days.

507
00:43:18,010 --> 00:43:23,180
When it's over, ice and snow 
will cover the entire 
Northern Hemisphere.

508
00:43:23,220 --> 00:43:26,730
The ice and snow 
will reflect sunlight.

509
00:43:26,810 --> 00:43:28,940
The earth's atmosphere 
will restabilize...

510
00:43:29,020 --> 00:43:33,740
but with an average 
temperature close to that 
of the last ice age.

511
00:43:33,780 --> 00:43:35,650
What can we do about this?

512
00:43:35,740 --> 00:43:39,200
[ Sighs ] 
Head as far south as possible.

513
00:43:39,240 --> 00:43:41,450
That is not amusing, 
Professor.

514
00:43:41,530 --> 00:43:44,200
Where do you suggest 
they go?

515
00:43:44,250 --> 00:43:47,080
The farther south they go, 
the safer they'll be.

516
00:43:47,120 --> 00:43:51,250
Texas. Parts of Florida 
that aren't flooded. 
Mexico would be best.

517
00:43:51,290 --> 00:43:55,420
Mexico? Maybe 
you should stick to science 
and leave policy to us.

518
00:43:55,470 --> 00:43:58,720
Well, we tried that approach. 
You didn't want to hear 
about the science...

519
00:43:58,760 --> 00:44:02,140
when it could 
have made a difference.

520
00:44:02,220 --> 00:44:05,930
What exactly are you 
proposing, Professor?

521
00:44:11,360 --> 00:44:16,650
Evacuate everyone 
south of that line.

522
00:44:20,490 --> 00:44:24,660
What about the people 
in the north?

523
00:44:26,750 --> 00:44:29,460
I'm afraid 
it's too late for them.

524
00:44:29,540 --> 00:44:34,300
If they go outside, 
the storm will kill them.

525
00:44:36,960 --> 00:44:39,550
At this point,

526
00:44:39,630 --> 00:44:42,800
their best chance 
is to stay inside,

527
00:44:42,890 --> 00:44:46,180
try to ride it out,

528
00:44:46,270 --> 00:44:48,350
pray.

529
00:44:52,360 --> 00:44:55,070
What do you think he'll do?

530
00:44:55,110 --> 00:44:57,280
I don't know.

531
00:44:57,320 --> 00:45:00,200
Jack, thanks.

532
00:45:00,280 --> 00:45:02,200
And good luck.

533
00:45:02,280 --> 00:45:04,330
You too.

534
00:45:06,080 --> 00:45:07,950
We're all gonna need it.

535
00:45:10,830 --> 00:45:13,580
[ Wind Howling ]

536
00:45:16,000 --> 00:45:18,710
[ Generator Chugging ]

537
00:45:27,890 --> 00:45:31,520
Sorry, mates, but we're 
just about out of petrol.

538
00:45:34,730 --> 00:45:38,400
Hey, is there any chance...

539
00:45:38,440 --> 00:45:42,530
that it will run... on this?

540
00:45:42,610 --> 00:45:45,410
[ Chuckles ] 
Are you mad?

541
00:45:45,450 --> 00:45:47,910
That's a 12-year-old scotch.

542
00:45:55,460 --> 00:45:57,800
[ Laughs ]

543
00:46:09,680 --> 00:46:12,890
Gentlemen. To England.

544
00:46:15,270 --> 00:46:17,440
To mankind.

545
00:46:18,690 --> 00:46:20,860
To Manchester United.

546
00:46:20,940 --> 00:46:23,490
[ All Laughing ]

547
00:46:45,930 --> 00:46:49,390
Come on, Buddha. Come on. 
Do your business.

548
00:46:49,470 --> 00:46:52,350
Look. There's nobody around.

549
00:46:52,390 --> 00:46:55,520
[ Buddha Whimpering ] 
Okay. You know what? 
I'll turn around. I won't look.

550
00:46:55,560 --> 00:46:58,060
I promise. 
[ Barking ]

551
00:46:58,150 --> 00:47:02,150
[ Barking Continues ] 
What?

552
00:47:08,160 --> 00:47:11,740
Hey, man. 
There's people out there. 
I was walking my dog.

553
00:47:11,830 --> 00:47:15,870
There's hundreds of 'em. 
They're walkin' on the snow.

554
00:47:19,380 --> 00:47:21,630
[ Chattering ]

555
00:47:23,670 --> 00:47:25,590
[ Officer ] 
Where are they all going?

556
00:47:25,680 --> 00:47:28,800
They're gettin' 
out of the city 
before it's too late.

557
00:47:31,310 --> 00:47:33,850
[ Wind Howling ]

558
00:47:59,630 --> 00:48:01,590
I got it.

559
00:48:01,630 --> 00:48:05,050
You're supposed to be 
on a bus heading south.

560
00:48:05,130 --> 00:48:07,930
I've been watching 
your back for 20 years.

561
00:48:07,970 --> 00:48:10,390
You think I'd let you 
go alone?

562
00:48:10,470 --> 00:48:13,350
All these years, I thought 
I was watching your back.

563
00:48:13,430 --> 00:48:17,060
Where are the keys? 
In the truck.

564
00:48:17,140 --> 00:48:19,560
Where do you think 
you're going?

565
00:48:19,650 --> 00:48:22,480
Neither one of you 
knows how to navigate 
worth a damn.

566
00:48:22,520 --> 00:48:24,610
Without me, you'll 
end up in Cleveland.

567
00:48:26,650 --> 00:48:30,070
I'll try to give you 
updates on the storm 
as it heads your way.

568
00:48:30,160 --> 00:48:32,830
Good luck, Jack.

569
00:48:32,910 --> 00:48:34,740
[ Engine Starts ]

570
00:48:45,960 --> 00:48:48,970
[ Woman Reporting ] 
Breaking news from 
the U.S.-Mexico border.

571
00:48:49,010 --> 00:48:52,430
Just half an hour ago, 
Mexican officials 
closed the border...

572
00:48:52,510 --> 00:48:56,390
in the light 
of so many U.S. refugees 
who are fleeing south...

573
00:48:56,470 --> 00:48:58,810
in the wake 
of the approaching storm.

574
00:48:58,850 --> 00:49:02,400
These people came here 
in anticipation of crossing 
into Mexico.

575
00:49:02,480 --> 00:49:05,270
Instead, they've been met 
with closed gates--

576
00:49:05,400 --> 00:49:07,360
Here it is.

577
00:49:07,440 --> 00:49:12,200
Oh, this fireplace 
probably hasn't been used 
in about a hundred years.

578
00:49:14,990 --> 00:49:17,200
All right.

579
00:49:22,380 --> 00:49:25,880
- What are you doing? 
- What do you think 
we were gonna burn?

580
00:49:25,960 --> 00:49:29,510
You can't burn books. 
No. Absolutely not!

581
00:49:29,550 --> 00:49:33,010
You wanna freeze to death?

582
00:49:34,220 --> 00:49:38,220
I'll go get some more. 
I'll help you.

583
00:49:38,310 --> 00:49:40,230
I'm going with them.

584
00:49:41,730 --> 00:49:44,940
Okay. Do you have 
a cafeteria or a lunchroom?

585
00:49:45,020 --> 00:49:47,530
Just an employees' lounge 
with a few vending machines.

586
00:49:47,570 --> 00:49:50,650
[ Buddha Barking ]

587
00:49:55,320 --> 00:49:57,910
We're not gonna last very long 
on M&M's and potato chips.

588
00:49:57,990 --> 00:50:01,830
What about the garbage cans? 
There's always somethin' 
to eat in the garbage.

589
00:50:01,870 --> 00:50:04,000
[ Buddha Whimpering ]

590
00:50:11,880 --> 00:50:14,590
[ Gasps ] 
Are you all right?

591
00:50:14,680 --> 00:50:17,720
Yeah. I'm fine. I just 
cut my leg the other day.

592
00:50:17,810 --> 00:50:21,180
[ Radio Static ]

593
00:50:21,230 --> 00:50:23,730
Did you get a signal?

594
00:50:23,810 --> 00:50:26,770
Yeah. For a minute. 
And?

595
00:50:30,860 --> 00:50:35,530
Man, this storm is everywhere. 
It's hit the entire 
Northern Hemisphere.

596
00:50:35,610 --> 00:50:39,290
Europe is buried 
under 15 feet of snow.

597
00:50:39,330 --> 00:50:41,700
And they say it's gonna 
get just as bad here.

598
00:50:41,790 --> 00:50:45,210
I mean, I don't think 
your dad's gonna make it.

599
00:50:48,710 --> 00:50:50,550
No. He'll make it.

600
00:50:54,930 --> 00:50:56,800
He'll make it.

601
00:51:00,770 --> 00:51:02,730
[ Man On News ] 
The White House has ordered...

602
00:51:02,810 --> 00:51:06,560
the following national disaster 
alert for all northern states:

603
00:51:06,600 --> 00:51:09,650
Continued exposure 
to the approaching storm 
is deadly.

604
00:51:09,730 --> 00:51:12,030
Remain indoors and take 
all measures to stay warm,

605
00:51:12,070 --> 00:51:14,530
burning any fuel sources 
until the storm is over.

606
00:51:15,490 --> 00:51:17,780
Roads are impassable 
across New York State...

607
00:51:17,870 --> 00:51:20,580
and as far south 
as Pennsylvania. 
Where are we?

608
00:51:20,620 --> 00:51:23,200
[ Man On News Continues, 
Indistinct ]

609
00:51:23,250 --> 00:51:26,580
Looks like we're 
just north of Philadelphia.

610
00:51:26,620 --> 00:51:29,710
[ Man On News ] 
Rescue workers say 
they have no idea--

611
00:51:29,750 --> 00:51:31,500
Look out!

612
00:51:34,130 --> 00:51:36,800
You guys okay?

613
00:51:36,880 --> 00:51:39,180
Yeah. 
Yeah.

614
00:51:44,770 --> 00:51:48,100
- Once again, roads across 
the northern states... 
- Sorry, boss.

615
00:51:48,190 --> 00:51:53,280
are impassable. 
Unpack the snowshoes. 
We're walking from here.

616
00:51:54,650 --> 00:51:57,910
Come on, men. 
Let's get up and get going.

617
00:51:57,950 --> 00:52:03,200
We've only made it to Brooklyn. 
Maybe we should just turn back.

618
00:52:03,290 --> 00:52:05,870
What for? Half the city's 
frozen underwater.

619
00:52:05,960 --> 00:52:08,750
There's nothing to go back to. 
It's time to get up. 
Keep moving.

620
00:52:08,790 --> 00:52:12,380
We should've stayed 
in the library. 
Come on, men.

621
00:52:12,460 --> 00:52:15,420
Okay, guys. 
Let's get moving.

622
00:52:24,680 --> 00:52:26,810
Wake up.

623
00:52:44,370 --> 00:52:48,250
What are you doing? 
Insulatin'.

624
00:52:48,330 --> 00:52:50,880
Newspaper's best, 
but this'll do it.

625
00:52:50,960 --> 00:52:54,960
You know, you spend 
some years on the streets, 
and you learn how to keep warm.

626
00:52:55,000 --> 00:52:57,920
Hey.

627
00:53:01,390 --> 00:53:03,300
Thanks.

628
00:53:42,760 --> 00:53:46,220
[ Grunting, Screaming ]

629
00:53:58,940 --> 00:54:01,150
[ Groaning, Whimpering ]

630
00:54:07,830 --> 00:54:09,790
[ Jack ] 
Frank!

631
00:54:09,870 --> 00:54:13,210
Are you all right? 
I'm fine!

632
00:54:15,000 --> 00:54:17,710
Just dropped in 
to do a little shopping.

633
00:54:17,750 --> 00:54:20,460
I gotta lose this sled.

634
00:54:37,440 --> 00:54:39,780
Hold on! 
We'll pull you up!

635
00:54:41,940 --> 00:54:45,200
Jason! You gotta 
support Frank's weight!

636
00:54:45,280 --> 00:54:47,410
Okay! I'll try!

637
00:54:50,240 --> 00:54:51,100
[ Grunting ]

638
00:54:54,120 --> 00:54:57,170
Okay! I think 
I have his weight now!

639
00:54:57,250 --> 00:54:59,000
I'm coming to you!

640
00:55:05,630 --> 00:55:07,930
[ Glass Cracking ]

641
00:55:08,010 --> 00:55:09,140
[ Grunting, Whimpering ]

642
00:55:10,970 --> 00:55:13,020
The glass is breaking!

643
00:55:15,980 --> 00:55:17,980
Too much weight!

644
00:55:21,780 --> 00:55:23,320
It's not gonna hold.

645
00:55:32,870 --> 00:55:34,460
No! No!

646
00:55:34,540 --> 00:55:37,830
Frank! 
I can get you out!

647
00:55:39,540 --> 00:55:41,710
[ Jack ] 
Don't do it!

648
00:55:41,750 --> 00:55:44,170
- No! Frank! Don't! 
- Frank, no!

649
00:55:45,720 --> 00:55:47,380
Frank!

650
00:56:29,840 --> 00:56:32,300
[ Radio: 
Chatter, Indistinct ]

651
00:56:32,350 --> 00:56:34,470
Get this convoy 
moving now.

652
00:56:36,770 --> 00:56:39,690
[ Whispering ] 
I know. I know.

653
00:56:39,730 --> 00:56:42,440
Mr. President. 
All right.

654
00:56:42,520 --> 00:56:45,440
I'm sorry, sir. 
We can't wait any longer.

655
00:56:45,530 --> 00:56:47,860
We're the last ones.

656
00:56:47,950 --> 00:56:49,860
All right.

657
00:57:05,210 --> 00:57:07,880
[ Pilot ] 
Mexicali Control, 
this is Delta 2-6.

658
00:57:07,920 --> 00:57:10,840
We're carrying an urgent message 
for high command.

659
00:57:10,880 --> 00:57:12,930
Request priority 
on landing zone one.

660
00:57:13,010 --> 00:57:14,140
[ Chattering, Shouting ] 
[ Radio Chatter Continues, 
Indistinct ]

661
00:57:21,270 --> 00:57:23,310
Sir.

662
00:57:23,400 --> 00:57:25,820
Thank you, Corporal. 
Carry on.

663
00:57:39,040 --> 00:57:40,910
Madam Secretary?

664
00:57:50,420 --> 00:57:52,760
Raymond.

665
00:57:52,800 --> 00:57:55,510
The president's motorcade 
got caught in the storm.

666
00:58:00,180 --> 00:58:02,100
They didn't make it.

667
00:58:07,940 --> 00:58:10,240
She wouldn't 
wake up this morning.

668
00:58:10,280 --> 00:58:13,610
I-I-- I mean, last night 
she only had a fever. 
I don't understand.

669
00:58:13,700 --> 00:58:15,620
God, she's awfully pale.

670
00:58:15,660 --> 00:58:18,620
Well, guys, look. 
none of us has had anything 
real to eat in days.

671
00:58:18,700 --> 00:58:21,910
[ Jeremy ] I'm telling you, 
it's hypothermia. 
How could it be hypothermia?

672
00:58:21,960 --> 00:58:24,170
We've all been-- 
Maybe it's just 
the flu, you guys.

673
00:58:24,250 --> 00:58:26,590
No, no, 
it's not the flu.

674
00:58:27,710 --> 00:58:29,880
Yeah? 
And how do you know?

675
00:58:29,960 --> 00:58:33,340
Books can be good for 
something other than burning. 
Let's go over her symptoms.

676
00:58:33,430 --> 00:58:36,930
I told you. She has a fever 
and a really cold sweat.

677
00:58:36,970 --> 00:58:39,060
[ Woman ] 
How's her pulse?

678
00:58:39,140 --> 00:58:40,970
It's really fast.

679
00:58:41,020 --> 00:58:44,350
Did she have any injuries, 
like a cut or something 
that might have gotten infected?

680
00:58:44,440 --> 00:58:48,110
She was complaining 
about a cut on her leg 
a few days ago.

681
00:58:48,150 --> 00:58:51,150
I didn't-- I didn't 
think anything of it.

682
00:58:55,660 --> 00:58:57,740
Oh, my gosh.

683
00:58:58,700 --> 00:59:00,740
That's blood poisoning.

684
00:59:00,790 --> 00:59:03,750
Um, septicemia. 
She could go into septic shock.

685
00:59:04,460 --> 00:59:06,670
I've seen that before. 
That can get bad.

686
00:59:06,710 --> 00:59:08,840
She needs a massive dose 
of penicillin...

687
00:59:08,840 --> 00:59:11,130
or a broad-spectrum 
antibiotic immediately,

688
00:59:11,130 --> 00:59:12,340
or--

689
00:59:17,340 --> 00:59:19,300
Or what?

690
00:59:39,700 --> 00:59:42,910
I've never seen 
anything like it. 
There's no point of reference.

691
00:59:42,100 --> 00:59:44,830
All I can see 
is cloud cover.

692
00:59:44,870 --> 00:59:46,870
What are you doing?

693
00:59:46,920 --> 00:59:50,210
Taking infrared image 
of thermal currents...

694
00:59:50,290 --> 00:59:52,710
sent to Houston, Korolev,

695
00:59:52,800 --> 00:59:54,760
your weather service.

696
00:59:54,840 --> 00:59:56,840
[ Parker ] 
I'll help you.

697
00:59:57,880 --> 00:59:59,930
Tom? 
Tom, wake up. 
[ Groans ]

698
01:00:00,010 --> 01:00:02,810
I just received 
some satellite images 
from the space station.

699
01:00:02,890 --> 01:00:04,930
You'd better take a look.

700
01:00:16,240 --> 01:00:17,070
How big is this thing?

701
01:00:18,070 --> 01:00:20,950
The vortex is 
50 miles in diameter 
and growing.

702
01:00:21,030 --> 01:00:23,450
The two cells 
over Europe and Asia 
are even bigger.

703
01:00:23,540 --> 01:00:25,330
[ Tom ] 
Good God.

704
01:00:25,410 --> 01:00:28,710
This one's gonna hit New York 
inside an hour.

705
01:00:31,080 --> 01:00:33,750
[ Gasping, Panting ] 
Jack--

706
01:00:44,510 --> 01:00:46,520
Jason!

707
01:00:46,600 --> 01:00:48,270
Are you all right?

708
01:00:48,350 --> 01:00:50,770
Jason!

709
01:00:54,940 --> 01:00:57,030
[ Panting, Gasping ]

710
01:00:59,780 --> 01:01:01,990
[ Barking ]

711
01:01:06,450 --> 01:01:09,920
- What are you doing? 
- There's gotta be medicine 
on that ship.

712
01:01:09,960 --> 01:01:12,540
I thought you said 
it was too dangerous 
to go outside.

713
01:01:12,630 --> 01:01:15,050
I know I did.

714
01:01:20,930 --> 01:01:22,720
Where'd you find 
those chairs?

715
01:01:34,520 --> 01:01:36,650
[ Gasps ]

716
01:02:21,280 --> 01:02:23,860
There!

717
01:02:23,910 --> 01:02:26,490
[ Lock Rattling ] 
Ohh! Come on!

718
01:02:26,580 --> 01:02:28,740
Damn it!

719
01:02:28,790 --> 01:02:31,250
- [ Grunts Loudly ] 
- [ Growling ]

720
01:02:35,750 --> 01:02:39,500
[ Grunting Continues ]

721
01:03:00,900 --> 01:03:02,900
[ Yells ]

722
01:03:06,070 --> 01:03:08,580
[ Panting ]

723
01:03:16,330 --> 01:03:19,040
- It's all in Russian. I-- 
- [ Sam ] I know.

724
01:03:19,090 --> 01:03:22,670
- H-H-Hey, guys, I found it. 
- What? How do you know?

725
01:03:22,760 --> 01:03:25,430
Because it says "penicillin" 
on the bottom.

726
01:03:25,470 --> 01:03:27,970
[ Growling ]

727
01:03:30,600 --> 01:03:34,480
Wait. This is the mess hall. 
We should find some food 
while we're here.

728
01:03:34,520 --> 01:03:38,690
We don't have time. 
None of us are gonna survive 
without food, including Laura.

729
01:03:39,610 --> 01:03:41,780
Okay.

730
01:03:43,440 --> 01:03:45,450
[ Exhales, Clears Throat ]

731
01:03:45,490 --> 01:03:47,530
[ J.D. ] 
Sam, over here!

732
01:03:47,610 --> 01:03:49,530
What?

733
01:03:49,620 --> 01:03:51,740
Bingo. 
[ Laughs ]

734
01:03:51,790 --> 01:03:53,830
[ Sam Laughs ]

735
01:03:53,870 --> 01:03:55,960
[ Glass Clattering ]

736
01:03:58,750 --> 01:04:02,250
[ Inflating Loudly ] 
[ Yelling ]

737
01:04:02,300 --> 01:04:04,380
[ Sam ] 
Brian?

738
01:04:04,460 --> 01:04:07,130
[ Brian ] 
I'm okay. 
[ J.D. ] What happened?

739
01:04:07,180 --> 01:04:10,720
Okay, okay. All I did 
was open up a cupboard.

740
01:04:10,800 --> 01:04:13,930
[ Quiet Snarling ] 
We can use it. 
Put food in it.

741
01:04:14,020 --> 01:04:15,850
[ Loud Snarl ] 
[ Gasps ]

742
01:04:15,890 --> 01:04:18,980
[ Growling ] 
[ Panicked Yelling ]

743
01:04:22,190 --> 01:04:25,360
- [ Continues Yelling ] 
- [ Grunting ]

744
01:04:26,610 --> 01:04:29,110
Pull him in! 
[ Wolf Whimpers ]

745
01:04:29,160 --> 01:04:32,200
[ Whimpering, Growling ] 
You all right?

746
01:04:36,500 --> 01:04:38,370
[ J.D. Groans ] 
My leg.

747
01:04:41,840 --> 01:04:43,250
Here, use this.

748
01:04:43,340 --> 01:04:46,920
[ Shuddering, Groaning ] 
Oh, yeah. Here. Ohh!

749
01:04:51,720 --> 01:04:54,220
It should be 
over New York by now.

750
01:04:54,310 --> 01:04:59,190
Satellite readings 
are showing a temperature drop 
of 10 degrees per second.

751
01:05:15,080 --> 01:05:18,710
[ Sam ] 
You guys, I think 
we're in the eye of it.

752
01:05:18,790 --> 01:05:21,790
We gotta get back right now.

753
01:05:23,040 --> 01:05:24,920
Look, 
I'm gonna go outside...

754
01:05:24,960 --> 01:05:28,090
and I'm gonna lure the wolves 
out of the room.

755
01:05:28,130 --> 01:05:31,430
- When they leave, 
you lock the door. 
- Good luck.

756
01:06:02,670 --> 01:06:04,290
- [ Crunch ] 
- [ Growling ]

757
01:06:06,630 --> 01:06:09,210
[ Yelling ]

758
01:06:09,260 --> 01:06:12,260
Aah! 
[ Gasps ]

759
01:06:12,300 --> 01:06:14,930
[ Muttering, Grunting ]

760
01:06:16,890 --> 01:06:19,140
[ Growling, Snarling ]

761
01:06:20,730 --> 01:06:23,400
Aah! 
[ Panting, Gasping ]

762
01:06:29,190 --> 01:06:32,150
- [ Wolf Whimpers ] 
- [ Panting ]

763
01:06:35,570 --> 01:06:38,910
[ Groans ] 
Brian, hurry!

764
01:06:38,990 --> 01:06:41,910
[ Muttering ] 
Come on! Hurry!

765
01:06:43,370 --> 01:06:45,540
[ Snarling, Growling ]

766
01:06:45,630 --> 01:06:48,710
[ Groans, Panting ]

767
01:06:48,800 --> 01:06:50,760
[ Growling Continues ] 
[ Banging On Door ]

768
01:06:52,590 --> 01:06:54,840
Brian! 
Brian, open the door!

769
01:06:59,310 --> 01:07:03,140
We have to get out of here now. 
Brian, get the supplies.

770
01:07:42,060 --> 01:07:44,480
[ Grunting, Panting ]

771
01:07:53,570 --> 01:07:56,030
[ Crackling ]

772
01:08:01,240 --> 01:08:02,830
Let's go!

773
01:08:02,910 --> 01:08:04,830
Pull! Pull, Brian!

774
01:08:30,060 --> 01:08:32,860
Come on! Pull!

775
01:08:40,870 --> 01:08:43,030
Take the medicine to Laura!

776
01:08:43,080 --> 01:08:45,100
[ J.D. Groaning, Yelling ]

777
01:09:14,480 --> 01:09:18,150
We're almost there, man! 
Come on! Aah! 
[ Grunts ]

778
01:09:18,190 --> 01:09:21,490
Brian, close the door!

779
01:09:32,630 --> 01:09:34,960
Don't let the fire 
go out!

780
01:09:35,000 --> 01:09:37,170
What's happening?

781
01:09:43,140 --> 01:09:46,510
Buddha, 
come away from the door!

782
01:09:46,600 --> 01:09:48,020
Come, Buddha, come!

783
01:09:48,060 --> 01:09:51,480
More books! More books!

784
01:10:45,160 --> 01:10:48,080
How long 
have I been out of it?

785
01:10:48,160 --> 01:10:50,100
Couple hours. 
How do you feel?

786
01:10:51,080 --> 01:10:52,790
Okay.

787
01:10:52,870 --> 01:10:54,830
What happened?

788
01:10:54,920 --> 01:10:59,630
Well, we had to get inside 
in kind of a hurry, 
so I sort of pushed you in.

789
01:11:00,760 --> 01:11:04,180
I should be used to 
you pushing me around.

790
01:11:04,260 --> 01:11:06,390
Good to have you back.

791
01:11:11,140 --> 01:11:13,230
What are you doing?

792
01:11:14,310 --> 01:11:16,440
The eye of the storm 
has passed,

793
01:11:16,520 --> 01:11:18,440
and we're 40 miles 
from Manhattan.

794
01:11:18,520 --> 01:11:20,610
Shouldn't we wait 
one more day?

795
01:11:23,280 --> 01:11:26,030
Sam may not have 
one more day.

796
01:12:37,190 --> 01:12:40,150
What do you think's 
gonna happen to us?

797
01:12:41,110 --> 01:12:43,190
What do you mean?

798
01:12:44,190 --> 01:12:46,530
I mean us. 
Civilization.

799
01:12:46,610 --> 01:12:48,570
Everybody.

800
01:12:50,700 --> 01:12:53,700
Mankind survived 
the last ice age.

801
01:12:53,740 --> 01:12:56,830
We're certainly capable 
of surviving this one.

802
01:12:58,660 --> 01:13:02,210
All depends on 
whether or not we're able 
to learn from our mistakes.

803
01:13:04,630 --> 01:13:07,880
I sure as hell would like 
a chance to learn from mine.

804
01:13:09,130 --> 01:13:11,430
You did everything you could.

805
01:13:14,430 --> 01:13:16,520
I was thinking about Sam.

806
01:13:22,440 --> 01:13:25,230
Jack, you know 
the chances of Sam--

807
01:13:36,990 --> 01:13:39,210
I made my son a promise.

808
01:13:40,790 --> 01:13:42,710
I'm going to keep it.

809
01:14:05,770 --> 01:14:09,320
[ Man On Radio ] 
Parker, this is Houston. 
Do you read?

810
01:14:09,400 --> 01:14:10,530
Roger, Mission Control. 
Go ahead.

811
01:14:11,450 --> 01:14:15,320
We're getting scattered reports 
that the storm is dissipating 
over North America.

812
01:14:15,410 --> 01:14:17,790
Can you confirm?

813
01:14:19,040 --> 01:14:22,370
[ Parker ] 
Affirmative. 
It's finally clearing.

814
01:14:23,420 --> 01:14:25,380
We're over Europe right now.

815
01:14:25,460 --> 01:14:28,460
I can see landmass 
for the first time in days.

816
01:16:17,530 --> 01:16:20,030
How much further is it 
to the library?

817
01:16:20,070 --> 01:16:21,910
It should be...

818
01:16:25,450 --> 01:16:27,580
right here.

819
01:16:31,920 --> 01:16:34,050
I'm sorry, Jack.

820
01:17:11,210 --> 01:17:13,380
Sam?

821
01:18:21,280 --> 01:18:24,160
Who is that?

822
01:18:30,160 --> 01:18:32,580
My father.

823
01:18:35,920 --> 01:18:38,210
You made it.

824
01:18:42,430 --> 01:18:44,550
Of course I did.

825
01:19:10,240 --> 01:19:13,120
[ Crowd Shouting ]

826
01:19:23,670 --> 01:19:26,760
[ Shouting Continues ]

827
01:19:34,350 --> 01:19:36,270
Mr. President.

828
01:19:39,190 --> 01:19:43,110
I just received 
a shortwave radio transmission 
from Jack Hall.

829
01:19:43,150 --> 01:19:45,570
He made it to New York.

830
01:19:45,610 --> 01:19:47,700
He says 
there are survivors.

831
01:19:49,370 --> 01:19:51,540
Thank you, Tom.

832
01:19:51,620 --> 01:19:53,750
That's--

833
01:19:54,620 --> 01:19:56,750
That's good news.

834
01:20:30,530 --> 01:20:33,870
Jack! 
It's good to see you!

835
01:20:33,950 --> 01:20:36,540
Come on. 
Let's go get on board.

836
01:21:31,220 --> 01:21:33,600
Look at that.

837
01:21:33,640 --> 01:21:35,560
What?

838
01:21:35,600 --> 01:21:38,640
Have you ever seen the air 
so clear?
